# Workspace Guide (Tier 1)

One Next.js app: the Roth conversion calculator. It also hosts Auth.js.
This is the only always-on guide. Everything else loads conditionally, so
keep this file short.

## How the guide system works

Three tiers of `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`, loaded by scope:

- **Tier 1, this file** (`alwaysApply: true`): standards and the protocol
  below. Always in context.
- **Tier 2, the Project Guide** (`.cursor/rules/project-guide.mdc`): stack,
  layout, invariants, and a feature index.
- **Tier 3, Feature Guides** (`.cursor/rules/features/<feature>.mdc`, narrow
  `globs`): the files a feature spans plus its invariants and gotchas. Loads
  only when you touch those files.

Keep exactly one `alwaysApply: true` file (this one). Everything else is
glob-scoped so it stays out of context until relevant.

## Global standards

**Next.js / TypeScript pinning.** Next is `16.2.7`, React 19, TypeScript 5.
This is not the Next.js in your training data: APIs, conventions, and file
structure differ. Read the relevant guide in `node_modules/next/dist/docs/`
before writing Next code, and heed deprecation notices.

**One app, one source for each constant.** Auth, invites, email, legal, and
shared UI live under `src/lib/auth` and `src/lib/common`. Import them. Do not
re-declare invite status or cookie names (`src/lib/auth/shared/inviteConstants.ts`).
Inside the app, use the `@/` path alias.

**Environment.** One `.env` at the project root (Auth.js, Mongo, Google,
email, `MONGODB_DB`, external-data). Next loads it. `.env.local`
overrides. See `.env.example`. Never put server-only keys (`MONGODB_*`,
`EXTERNAL_DATA_*`) in `nextConfig.env`. Do not set `turbopack.root` to a
parent folder: that makes the compiler watch `node_modules` and `.next` and
can freeze the machine.

**Local app / IDE login.** The app is `http://localhost:3000`. The Cursor
browser is a separate cookie jar: do not try Google OAuth, a magic-link email,
or CDP cookie injection. To see signed-in UI, navigate to
`http://localhost:3000/api/dev/login?email=...`. The email is required and must
match `DEV_LOGIN_EMAIL` in `.env`. After landing, open `/{planId}` if you have
an id. If that account is a superAdmin, **View as user…** is the second hop
for another person's plan. Writes are live. Do not invent a new login path.
Gates: [`docs/dev-login.md`](../../docs/dev-login.md).

**Email.** Never call the Mailgun API directly. Send through `sendEmail`
(`@/lib/common/server/email`), `sendMail`, or `sendSupportMail`
(`@/lib/auth/server`). Every message to users sets Reply-To to the support
inbox. Only support-inbox forwards use `sendSupportMail`.

**Legal documents.** When you change Terms or Privacy content in
`src/lib/common/client`, you MUST in the same change bump `TERMS_VERSION` /
`PRIVACY_VERSION` (and the matching `*_EFFECTIVE_DATE`) and run
`npm run invalidate-sessions`. Never edit legal copy without bumping version
and effective date.

**Deploy / social previews.** Absolute `og:image` URLs always point at the
canonical domain `https://thewealthlab.ai` (see `@/lib/common/site`), never a
`*.vercel.app` host.

**Shipping to main.** Pushes on `claude/**` merge into `main` after 120
seconds of quiet. Each new push on that branch cancels the in-progress wait
and the 120 seconds start over. The workflow then runs tests and merges that
commit into `main` (`.github/workflows/auto-merge-claude.yml`).

**Writing.** UI copy, comments, and docs are friendly and informal: warm,
conversational, human. Contractions are fine (`we're`, `you'll`, `don't`).
Never use a long dash (em dash). Never use a hyphen as a sentence separator
(prefer a colon, comma, or rephrase). Hyphens inside words or compound
adjectives are fine (`year-by-year`).

## Reading and update protocol

Follow this on every task. It is what keeps context lean and the guides
trustworthy.

1. This Workspace Guide is always in effect.
2. Read the Project Guide when the task touches the app.
3. From the Project Guide's feature index, read ONLY the Feature Guide(s) for
   the feature you're touching.
4. After a meaningful change, UPDATE the relevant guide (and the feature index
   when you add or remove a feature).
5. If you spent significant effort figuring something out (a gotcha, a hidden
   contract, a debugging insight), write it down at the narrowest relevant tier.
6. Keep every guide short and high-signal. Long guides defeat conditional
   loading.
